  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q8-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Whenever Eva writes thirteen integers on a page, she notices that at least four of these leave the same remainder when divided by 4. Explain why this should be so.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "There are four possible remainders: $0,1,2,3$.\n\nSince $13=4\\times3+1$, the generalised pigeonhole principle guarantees that at least four integers have the same remainder.",
      "worked": "Group the integers by their remainders when divided by $4$: $0,1,2,3$. These are four pigeonholes.\n\nIf each group contained at most three integers, there would be at most $4\\times3=12$ integers. There are thirteen integers, so at least one group contains four or more. These integers leave the same remainder when divided by $4.$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 32
  },
